{{also|Alliteration|allitération}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|NL.|allīterātiō}}, from {{m|la|allīterātus}}, from {{m|la|allīterō}}, from {{der|en|la|ad|t=to, towards, near}} and {{m|la|lītera|t=a letter}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˌlɪtəˈɹeɪʃən/|[əˌlɪɾəˈɹeɪʃən]|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=5}}
* {{audio|en|En-ca-alliteration.oga|Audio (CAN)}}

===Noun===
{{examples|sense=repetition of initial consonants|examples=* Behemoth, biggest born of earth, upheaved His vastness. ({{w|Milton}})
* Fly o'er waste fens and windy fields. ({{w|Alfred Tennyson}})
}}
{{examples|sense=repetition of consonants in accented word parts|examples=* In a somer seson whan soft was the sonne, I shope me in shroudes as I a shepe were. ({{w|Piers Plowman}})
}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# The repetition of consonant sounds at the beginning of two or more words immediately succeeding each other, or at short intervals.
#* {{quote-web|en|date=March 20 2018|url=http://www.itv.com/news/border/2018-03-20/fish-fury-flares-over-brussels-brexit-deal/|title=Fish fury flares over Brussels Brexit deal|work=ITV|passage=So fish fury all round, as there has been in the past. And as an aside, that '''alliteration''' was, sadly, not mine that of a former political correspondent of the Daily Record, John Deans, and applied to the 'cod wars' with Iceland.}}
# The recurrence of the same letter in accented parts of words, as in Anglo-Saxon alliterative meter.

